+# Lansing Codes Slack Invite Redirect Contributing Guide
+
+Thanks for helping people find the Lansing Codes Slack community. Please read
+our [Code of Conduct](https://www.lansing.codes/code-of-conduct/) before
+contributing.
+
+## Contents
+
+- [Code of Conduct](#code-of-conduct)
+- [Issue Reporting Guidelines](#issue-reporting-guidelines)
+- [Pull Request Guidelines](#pull-request-guidelines)
+- [Development Setup](#development-setup)
+- [Testing Locally](#testing-locally)
+- [Deployments](#deployments)
+
+## Code of Conduct
+
+The [Lansing Codes Code of Conduct](https://www.lansing.codes/code-of-conduct/)
+applies to issues, pull requests, and community discussions.
+
+## Issue Reporting Guidelines
+
+Check [open issues](https://github.com/lansingcodes/slackin-redirect/issues)
+before filing a new one. For a broken redirect, include the URL you visited,
+the response status and destination you observed, your browser, and the time of
+the test. Do not paste private invite links or account credentials into issues.
+For a feature request, explain the visitor problem and the expected behavior.
+
+## Pull Request Guidelines
+
+1. Discuss significant behavior changes in an issue before implementing them.
+2. Create a branch from `main` in your fork and keep the change focused.
+3. Update both `_redirects` and the fallback references in `index.html` when
+   changing the Slack invite. Keep the destinations identical.
+4. Describe what changed, link the issue, and include the exact local checks
+   and results in your pull request.
+5. Request maintainer review. A maintainer decides when to merge into `main`.
+
+If your change depends on another pull request, name that dependency and use
+the dependency branch as your base until it is merged. Do not include API keys,
+Slack tokens, or other secrets in a commit.
+
+## Development Setup
+
+You need [Git](https://git-scm.com/downloads), a text editor, and a browser.
+[Node.js and npm](https://nodejs.org/) are needed for the `live-server` command
+below; this repository has no application dependencies or build step. An editor
+with HTML highlighting, such as [VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/), is
+helpful.
+
+Fork [this repository](https://github.com/lansingcodes/slackin-redirect),
+then clone your fork and create a branch:
+
+```sh
+git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/slackin-redirect.git
+cd slackin-redirect
+git switch -c fix/describe-your-change
+```
+
+## Testing Locally
+
+From the repository root, start a static preview:
+
+```sh
+npx --yes live-server --no-browser --port=8080
+```
+
+Visit `http://127.0.0.1:8080/` in a browser. The HTML fallback should send you
+to the same Slack invite as its clickable link. Stop the server with Ctrl+C.
+`live-server` serves static files and does not apply Netlify's `_redirects`
+rule. Check the rule separately by comparing its destination to both URLs in
+`index.html`. For redirect behavior, use a Netlify deploy preview when one is
+available and check that `/` returns HTTP 302 with the expected `Location`.
+
+If you are changing only documentation, check that Markdown links and commands
+point to files and tools that exist. No unit test suite is configured for this
+static repository.
+
+## Deployments
+
+Submit a pull request to `main`. After review and merge, Netlify's Git
+integration deploys the `main` branch to the site behind `slack.lansing.codes`.
+Review the Netlify deployment result and verify the live redirect before
+considering the change complete. Contributors should not push directly to
+`main` or change production Netlify settings without a maintainer's direction.
